# Tab completion erroring internally (e.g. completing right after the `.` in
# `$totally_undefined.`, when `$totally_undefined` isn't bound to anything --
# the completion engine fails to resolve it with an "Unknown value" error, see
# fetch_value/parse.rs) must not discard the line being edited. It used to: the
# rustyline Completer's error path returned `ReadlineError::Interrupted`, which
# the outer interactive loop treats exactly like Ctrl-C -- printing "^C" and
# starting a fresh, empty prompt, discarding whatever had been typed so far.
#
# The rest of the line typed after the tab keystroke (`exists`) only ends up part
# of the same submitted command -- producing a distinctive "Unknown variable"
# error, since `$totally_undefined` itself was never declared -- if the line
# genuinely survived the tab intact. If it had been cleared instead, `exists`
# would be submitted as its own fresh, unrelated command (and fail a different
# way: an external/builtin command lookup, not a variable lookup).
source tests/pty_test_helpers.crush
$t := $(pty_spawn)
$n := $(pty_enable_counted_prompt $t)

$n = $(pty_run $t $n "$totally_undefined.\texists")
$t.wait_for "Unknown variable"

$t.close
